  "androidShellRoot": "Root (KernelSU / su)",
  "androidShellRootHint": "Starts an independent shell with su from KernelSU, Magisk, or APatch. Child /system/bin/sh jobs run as UID 0. This is off until you choose Root, read the high-risk notice, turn the switch on, and tap Connect. The first su can wait on the KernelSU grant for about 30 seconds. After connect, the real UID is checked and anything other than 0 is refused. The su process may stay up so KernelSU is not prompted on every command; each command still needs an in-app confirmation that shows the full command and the identity root, UID 0. Screen-control waivers do not apply. There is no automatic retry and no fallback to ADB or Shizuku, and su is never started from Ubuntu. This install is a new app and needs its own KernelSU grant.",
  "androidShellRootRiskTitle": "Allow system commands through su as root?",
  "androidShellRootRiskBody": "This starts an Android shell as UID 0 with su from KernelSU, Magisk, or APatch. Commands can change the system and app data. It stays off until you choose Root, turn the switch on, and tap Connect. If the connected UID is not 0, commands are refused. Every command still asks for confirmation and shows the full command with the identity root, UID 0. Screen-control waivers, including allow-for-this-task and always-allow, do not apply. A failure is not retried and is not silently sent through ADB or Shizuku.",
  "androidShellRootRiskAction": "I understand, use Root",
  "androidShellRootConnect": "Connect Root (su)",
  "androidShellRootConnected": "Root shell connected",
  "androidShellRootSkip": "Always skip confirmation",
  "androidShellRootSkipHint": "Root (KernelSU / su) only. While this is on, commands on a connected UID 0 channel run without a confirmation card. ADB and Shizuku still confirm every command. Screen-control waivers still do not apply.",
  "androidShellRootSkipOnNote": "Always skip confirmation is on for Root: UID 0 commands run directly. ADB and Shizuku still confirm every command.",
  "androidShellRootSkipRiskTitle": "Allow Root commands without confirmation?",
  "androidShellRootSkipRiskBody": "Root can change the system and app data. While this is on, a connected Root channel whose real UID is 0 runs commands without a confirmation card. Android Shell must stay enabled and Root must stay connected. Turning this off immediately restores per-command confirmation.",
  "androidShellRootSkipRiskAction": "Always skip confirmation",
  "androidShellAllowConcurrent": "Allow commands to run at the same time",
  "androidShellAllowConcurrentHint": "Off by default, so only one command runs. When on, Android Shell commands from different sessions run at the same time instead of waiting or returning busy. Commands can interfere with each other. This does not skip Root authorization, per-command confirmation, or the permission switches. Turning it off lets commands that already started finish; later commands run one at a time again.",
  "androidShellAllowConcurrentRiskTitle": "Allow commands to run at the same time?",
  "androidShellAllowConcurrentRiskBody": "Android Shell commands from different sessions can then run together. The app does not check whether those commands overwrite each other's files or contend for the same resource. Root, ADB, and Shizuku authorization and confirmation still apply. Turning this off does not stop a command that has already started.",
